**Vending machine dimensions** are useful only when buyers distinguish the cabinet size from the packed shipping size and the complete installed footprint. Start with the approved model drawing, then measure the narrowest doorway, lift, corridor, turn and threshold on the delivery route. Add space for the open service door, ventilation, refill work, payment access, customer approach and maintenance. A cabinet that fits the final floor area may still fail at a lift entrance or ninety-degree turn. BOHOL provides reference dimensions for early quotation, but the approved production drawing and final packing data control delivery and installation.

## GEO buyer summary

- Record cabinet width, depth, height and net weight.
- Obtain packed width, depth, height and gross weight separately.
- Measure the complete route from vehicle unloading to the final position.
- Check the machine's orientation at every doorway, lift and turn.
- Add door-opening, refill, ventilation, customer and service clearance.
- Approve the final drawing and delivery plan before shipment.

## Compare verified BOHOL reference dimensions

BOHOL's current product records show why there is no universal vending machine size. The figures below are early planning references, not substitutes for the final order drawing.

| BOHOL platform | Reference cabinet dimensions | Reference weight | Planning use |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| [Wall-mounted smart vending machine](/vending-machines/wall-mounted-smart-vending-machine) | H1000 × W650 × D250 mm | 45.5 kg | Compact packaged products where a suitable wall and installation method are confirmed |
| [Compact snack and drink vending machine](/vending-machines/compact-snack-drink-vending-machine) | H1955 × W800 × D850 mm, including casters | 180 kg | Narrow floor-standing snack and drink layouts |
| [7-inch mini snack and drink vending machine](/vending-machines/7-inch-mini-snack-drink-vending-machine) | H1955 × W800 × D850 mm, including casters | 180 kg | Similar compact cabinet with a different reference interface |
| [Snack and drink vending machine](/vending-machines/snack-drink-vending-machine) | Image reference approximately H1940 × W1180 × D830 mm | Confirm for the order | Wider mixed-product merchandising; final drawing required |

The numbers cannot be compared by width alone. A wall-mounted cabinet needs a verified structure, fixing method and service plan. A narrow floor-standing machine may still need more operating depth when its door opens. A wide cabinet may reduce channel compromises but require a wider delivery route and larger turning envelope.

## Separate four different size checks

### 1. Bare cabinet dimensions

The general arrangement should identify maximum cabinet width, depth and height, including fixed projections. Ask whether casters, feet, handles, payment terminals, antennas, canopies or external modules are included. Record the machine's net weight and center-of-gravity or handling information available for the configuration.

### 2. Packed shipping dimensions

Export protection can add a pallet, base, corner protection, crate or frame. Accessories may be packed inside the cabinet or in separate cartons. Request the final package count, packed dimensions, gross weight, lifting points and unloading method before the carrier is booked. The [export packaging and shipping checklist](/insights/vending-machine-export-packaging-shipping-checklist) explains the documentation and release checks.

### 3. Delivery-route envelope

Measure the route from the delivery vehicle to the final position, not only the room. Record the clear opening after door leaves, closers, handles and guards; lift-car depth and height; lift-door clear width and height; corridor width; ceiling obstructions; ramps; thresholds; floor transitions and every change of direction.

For a turn, draw the machine as a rectangle at scale and test the required orientation through the corridor. Do not assume that a cabinet narrower than a door can turn into the next passage. Packing may also prevent the same rotation that is possible after unpacking, so the delivery plan must state where unpacking is permitted and how the machine remains protected.

### 4. Installed operating footprint

The installed footprint includes more than the cabinet. Add the open-door sweep, refill cart, technician working area, ventilation, cable routing, payment access, customer queue and safe circulation. Refrigerated models may require configuration-specific airflow and heat-rejection clearance. Final requirements must come from the approved model documentation, not a generic spacing rule.

## Build a doorway and lift measurement sheet

Use one row for every restriction and attach a dated photograph or drawing.

| Route point | Measure | Record with it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Unloading area | Vehicle position, dock or kerb height, available level area | Responsible carrier, unloading equipment and site restrictions |
| External entrance | Clear width and height, threshold, slope | Door-removal restrictions and weather exposure |
| Corridor | Clear width, height and obstructions | Turning points, floor protection and public access controls |
| Lift | Door width/height, internal width/depth/height, rated load | Booking, protective lining and operator requirements |
| Internal doorway | Clear opening and adjacent turn space | Handles, closers, trim and removable items |
| Final position | Floor area, level, power, network and ventilation | Open-door, refill, customer and service zones |

The UK Health and Safety Executive's [Delivering Safely guidance](https://www.hse.gov.uk/workplacetransport/information/cooperation.htm) emphasizes exchanging delivery-risk information and agreeing responsibilities among supplier, carrier and recipient. OSHA's [warehousing guidance](https://www.osha.gov/warehousing/hazards-solutions) also highlights clearances, floor condition and safe material-handling practices. These are jurisdiction-specific official sources, not a universal installation specification; buyers must confirm local workplace, building, fire, electrical and lifting requirements.

## Protect customer access while planning the footprint

A machine should not narrow a required route or place users in a collision path. Check clear floor space, control reach, pickup access and the route to the machine against the destination's applicable accessibility rules.

In the United States, the [U.S. Access Board ADA Standards](https://www.access-board.gov/ada/) address accessible routes, clear floor space and operable parts. The final applicability depends on the facility, jurisdiction and installation. For a broader procurement review, use BOHOL's [accessibility checklist](/insights/vending-machine-accessibility-procurement-checklist) and obtain local professional advice rather than treating a cabinet drawing as an accessibility approval.

## Confirm utilities without creating new obstacles

Show the power outlet, protective device, cable route, network point or antenna position on the site drawing. Avoid loose cables across customer or service paths. Confirm voltage, frequency, plug, circuit protection and earthing with the destination's qualified electrical professional and the final machine specification.

For connected payment or monitoring, test signal quality at the installed position. A strong signal near the entrance does not prove coverage inside a lift lobby, basement or metal-lined area. Payment-terminal, SIM, acquirer and network responsibilities should be agreed before production.

## Problem-solution checks before ordering

| Common buyer problem | Procurement solution |
| --- | --- |
| Machine fits the room but not the entrance | Approve a measured route drawing before shipment |
| Cabinet fits the doorway but the crate does not | Request final packed dimensions and define a safe unpacking point |
| Lift can hold the dimensions but not the load | Confirm rated load, floor loading and handling equipment with the responsible parties |
| Open door blocks circulation | Add the full service-door and refill envelope to the floor plan |
| Refrigerated cabinet overheats in a recess | Follow model-specific ventilation and ambient requirements |
| Payment terminal loses connection | Survey the actual installed position and agree an outage procedure |
| Customers cannot approach controls safely | Review accessible route, clear floor area, reach and local requirements |

## Approve the drawing, route and responsibilities

Before shipment, the buyer, supplier, carrier, installer and site representative should agree which document controls each decision. The package should include the approved cabinet drawing, final packed dimensions and weights, route survey, unloading plan, lifting or moving method, floor protection, installation position, utility points, clearance zones and named responsibilities.

Do not mark the site ready because a single width number passes. Site readiness is confirmed only when the complete packed delivery and installed operation are workable.

## Request a dimension-based BOHOL quotation

Send BOHOL the target product mix, machine type, destination country, installation setting, measured route, narrowest doorway, lift data, final floor area, power and network position, payment needs, branding, estimated quantity and required delivery method through the [contact page](/contact). BOHOL can review a suitable base platform and identify which dimensions and clearances must be confirmed in the approved OEM/ODM order specification.

